 "LISTENING_VIEWPOINTS::Viewpoints — Should children have smartphones?": {
  "title": "Viewpoints — Should children have smartphones?",
  "prompt": "You will hear a longer talk presenting different viewpoints. It plays once. Choose the best option for each question.",
  "guidanceNote": "The speaker's own view often follows 'what researchers stress' — listen past the two sides for the conclusion.",
  "payload": {
   "audioScript": "Few questions divide parents more than when, or whether, to give a child a smartphone. Those in favour often point to safety: a phone means a child can reach a parent after school or call for help if plans change. Others worry less about the age and more about the effect — time lost to games and social apps, and the pressure of being reachable at all hours. What researchers tend to stress, though, is that the device itself matters less than the rules around it. A phone with clear limits on apps and screen time looks very different from one with none. So perhaps the more useful question is not 'how old should a child be?' but 'what boundaries come with the phone?'",
   "speakers": [
    {
     "role": "Speaker",
     "voice": "fable"
    }
   ],
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "stem": "What do parents in favour of giving children phones mainly point to?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "Better performance at school."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "Saving money on a landline."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "Helping children keep up with homework and school projects at home."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Safety and being able to reach the child."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "d",
     "explanation": "\"Those in favour often point to safety: a phone means a child can reach a parent.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q2",
     "stem": "According to the speaker, what matters more than the child's age?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "The rules and limits set around the phone."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "The brand of the phone."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "How much the phone costs."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "How many of the child's friends at school already have their own phone."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "a",
     "explanation": "\"The device itself matters less than the rules around it.\""
    }
   ]
  }
 },
 "LISTENING_VIEWPOINTS::Viewpoints — Online versus in-person learning": {
  "title": "Viewpoints — Online versus in-person learning",
  "prompt": "You will hear a longer talk presenting different viewpoints. It plays once. Choose the best option for each question.",
  "guidanceNote": "When a talk presents two sides, the speaker's verdict usually arrives at the end — here, after 'what they more often find'.",
  "payload": {
   "audioScript": "Since online courses became common, people have argued about whether they can match a classroom. Supporters of online learning highlight its flexibility: you can study at your own pace, fit lessons around a job, and reach courses that aren't offered nearby. Critics counter that something is lost without a room full of people — the questions that come up in live discussion, the motivation of sitting beside others working toward the same goal, the quick help a teacher gives by reading a confused face. Interestingly, the studies that compare the two rarely declare a clear winner. What they more often find is that the best results come from blending them: live sessions for discussion and feedback, online materials for the parts learners can work through alone. The format, in the end, may matter less than the mix.",
   "speakers": [
    {
     "role": "Speaker",
     "voice": "alloy"
    }
   ],
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "stem": "What advantage of online learning does the speaker note?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "It is always cheaper than a classroom."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "It removes the need for any teacher."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "It always gives better results than a classroom for every type of learner."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Its flexibility — studying at your own pace and around a job."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "d",
     "explanation": "\"Supporters of online learning highlight its flexibility: you can study at your own pace, fit lessons around a job.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q2",
     "stem": "What does the speaker conclude the studies tend to find?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "In-person learning always wins."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "Blending the two formats gives the best results."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "Online learning always wins."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Most students now prefer online courses to classroom ones."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "b",
     "explanation": "\"The best results come from blending them … The format, in the end, may matter less than the mix.\""
    }
   ]
  }
 },
