 "READING_VIEWPOINTS::Viewpoints — Cash or cashless?": {
  "title": "Viewpoints — Cash or cashless?",
  "prompt": "Read the passage, which presents different opinions, and answer the questions by choosing the best option.",
  "guidanceNote": "Even when two writers disagree, they may share a final position — read past the debate to the 'they agree' line.",
  "payload": {
   "passages": [
    {
     "id": "p1",
     "heading": "Two views on going cashless",
     "body": "As more shops put up 'card only' signs, two reactions have grown louder. Reza defends cash. He points out that not everyone has a bank card or a smartphone — older people and those on low incomes can be shut out when cash is refused — and he values the privacy of a payment that leaves no record. Bianca sees the convenience differently: tapping a card is faster, you don't have to carry change, and a business handling no cash is less of a target for theft. Yet for all their disagreement, the two land in a similar place. A shop that quietly drops cash, they agree, risks turning away the very customers least able to switch — so the fairest path is to keep accepting both for as long as people still rely on cash."
    }
   ],
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "What is Reza's main concern about going cashless?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "It can shut out people without a card or smartphone, and it reduces privacy."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "It is slower at the checkout."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "It makes shops a bigger target for theft."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Shops lose money because counting and storing coins takes too much staff time."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "a",
     "explanation": "\"Not everyone has a bank card or a smartphone … and he values the privacy of a payment that leaves no record.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q2",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "What do Reza and Bianca ultimately agree is the fairest path?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "Going fully cashless as soon as possible."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "Letting each customer pay a fee to use cash."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "Continuing to accept both cash and cards while people still rely on cash."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Accepting cash only from older customers and asking everyone else to pay by card."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "c",
     "explanation": "\"The fairest path is to keep accepting both for as long as people still rely on cash.\""
    }
   ]
  }
 },
