     "body": "Few desserts are as closely tied to Canada as the butter tart. This small pastry, filled with a mixture of butter, sugar, syrup, and egg, has been baked in Canadian kitchens for well over a century.\n\nOne of the earliest known printed recipes appeared in a cookbook from a women's group in Ontario in 1900. Because the ingredients were simple and affordable, the tart quickly became a favourite in rural homes, where farm kitchens usually had butter, eggs, and syrup on hand.\n\nToday the butter tart sparks friendly debate. The biggest argument is about texture: some bakers prefer a runny, almost liquid centre, while others insist a proper tart should be firm enough to hold its shape. A second question is whether to add raisins, which divides families and even entire towns.\n\nSeveral communities now hold butter tart festivals each summer, drawing thousands of visitors who sample dozens of versions in a single afternoon. Bakers compete for prizes, and the winning recipes are guarded closely. What everyone agrees on is that the little tart, once a thrifty farm treat, has become a small national treasure."
