     "body": "When a heavy snowfall blankets the city, drivers often wonder why their own street is still buried while the main road nearby has already been plowed. The answer lies in a clear system of priorities that crews follow every winter.\n\nFirst in line are the major routes: highways, bus corridors, and the streets leading to hospitals and fire halls. These carry the most traffic and serve emergencies, so keeping them open protects the greatest number of people. Crews aim to have them cleared within a few hours of a storm ending.\n\nNext come collector roads, the medium-sized streets that funnel cars from neighbourhoods toward the main routes. Only after these are passable do plows turn to quiet residential streets, which is why side streets are usually last.\n\nThis order sometimes frustrates residents, but reversing it would leave ambulances and buses stranded on unplowed highways. The city asks drivers to be patient, to move parked cars off the street when a snow route is declared, and to check the online plow tracker, which shows in real time where each truck has been and where it is headed next."
