     "id": "p1",
     "heading": "Let Teenagers Sleep",
     "body": "Each school morning, our high school opens its doors at 8:00, which means many teenagers are dragging themselves out of bed before dawn. As a teacher of fifteen years, I believe our board should push the start time to 9:00, and I am not alone.\n\nThe science on teenage sleep is well established. During these years, the body's internal clock shifts later, so teens naturally fall asleep and wake up later than younger children or adults. Fighting that clock does not make them lazy; it makes them tired. I see the results firsthand: students yawning through first period, unable to absorb a lesson their rested afternoon selves would grasp easily.\n\nI know the objection well. A later start complicates bus schedules and after-school jobs and sports. These are real hurdles, and I do not dismiss them. Yet other districts have moved their start times successfully by staggering bus routes and shifting practice times, so the problems are solvable with planning.\n\nWe ask a great deal of our students. The least we can do is let their school day begin when their minds are actually awake. An hour of sleep is not a luxury; for a growing teenager, it is a foundation for learning."
