 "READING_VIEWPOINTS::Viewpoints — A commentary on turning empty lots into community gardens": {
  "title": "Viewpoints — A commentary on turning empty lots into community gardens",
  "prompt": "Read the text, then answer the questions by choosing the best option.",
  "guidanceNote": "Separate the benefits the writer lists from the concern he raises and how he answers it.",
  "payload": {
   "passages": [
    {
     "id": "p1",
     "heading": "Let the Vacant Lots Grow",
     "body": "There is a weedy, fenced-off lot two blocks from my home in Hamilton that has sat empty for as long as I can remember. It collects litter, and no one gives it a second glance. I keep thinking the same thing every time I pass: what a waste.\n\nAcross the country, cities are turning lots just like it into community gardens, and I believe ours should follow. The benefits go well beyond a few rows of tomatoes. Neighbours who once nodded politely now spend Saturday mornings side by side, trading seeds and stories. Children learn where food actually comes from. Fresh vegetables reach families who might otherwise pay a premium for them at the store.\n\nCritics warn that gardens are hard to maintain and may be abandoned once the novelty fades. That is a fair concern, but the solution is not to give up on the idea; it is to plan for it. Gardens that thrive tend to have a small volunteer committee and a modest annual budget for water and tools.\n\nAn empty lot grows nothing but weeds. A garden grows food, friendships, and a little more pride in the place we call home."
    }
   ],
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "What does the writer propose for the empty lot?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "Building a parking lot on it."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "Leaving it fenced off as it is."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "Turning it into a community garden."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Selling it to a developer to build new houses."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "c",
     "explanation": "\"Cities are turning lots just like it into community gardens, and I believe ours should follow.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q2",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "According to the writer, what benefit goes beyond growing vegetables?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "Neighbours connecting and children learning about food."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "Higher property taxes for the city."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "More space for parked cars."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "Lower food prices at the supermarket."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "a",
     "explanation": "\"Neighbours … now spend Saturday mornings side by side … Children learn where food actually comes from.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q3",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "What concern do critics raise?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "That gardens grow too much food."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "That children dislike gardening."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "That gardens attract litter."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "That gardens are hard to maintain and may be abandoned."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "d",
     "explanation": "\"Critics warn that gardens are hard to maintain and may be abandoned once the novelty fades.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "q4",
     "kind": "mcq",
     "stem": "How does the writer answer that concern?",
     "options": [
      {
       "id": "a",
       "text": "He agrees the idea should be dropped."
      },
      {
       "id": "b",
       "text": "He says planning, such as a volunteer committee and a small budget, keeps gardens thriving."
      },
      {
       "id": "c",
       "text": "He ignores the concern entirely."
      },
      {
       "id": "d",
       "text": "He says the city should simply hire full-time gardeners to look after every community garden."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "b",
     "explanation": "\"Gardens that thrive tend to have a small volunteer committee and a modest annual budget for water and tools.\""
    }
   ]
  }
 },
